{"name":"Siam Gold","hex":"#896f40","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"siam","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move aside; to get out of the way.","To leave hastily, to run off."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Out of the way!; get lost!"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/siam"},{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"}],"entries":[{"title":"Thailand","blocks":[{"text":"Thailand, officially the Kingdom of Thailand, and formerly known as Siam, is a country located in Mainland Southeast Asia. It shares land borders with Myanmar to the west and northwest, Laos to the east and northeast, Cambodia to the southeast, and Malaysia to the south. Its maritime boundaries include the Gulf of Thailand and the Andaman Sea, and it shares maritime borders with Vietnam, Indonesia, and India. Thailand has a population of nearly 66 million people and covers an area of approximately 513,115 km2. The capital and largest city is Bangkok."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Flag_of_Thailand.svg/330px-Flag_of_Thailand.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thailand"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Dvaravati-style_Chedi_at_Wat_Phra_Rub%2C_Suphan_Buri_Province.jpg/330px-Dvaravati-style_Chedi_at_Wat_Phra_Rub%2C_Suphan_Buri_Province.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Dvaravati-style_Chedi_at_Wat_Phra_Rub%2C_Suphan_Buri_Province.jpg/330px-Dvaravati-style_Chedi_at_Wat_Phra_Rub%2C_Suphan_Buri_Province.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Dvaravati-style Chedi at Wat Phra Rub [th], Suphan Buri province, now completely destroyed.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADvaravati-style_Chedi_at_Wat_Phra_Rub%2C_Suphan_Buri_Province.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%84%E0%B9%8C%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%A2%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%942.jpg/500px-%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%84%E0%B9%8C%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%A2%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%942.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%84%E0%B9%8C%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A